---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import { links, url } from '../lib/links';

const questions = [
  { area: 'Sales', items: ['Which customers ordered less this quarter than last?', 'What is still open on our ten largest orders, and when is it due?'] },
  { area: 'Purchasing', items: ['Which purchase orders are late, and from which suppliers?', 'Which items have we bought from more than one supplier this year, and at what prices?'] },
  { area: 'Service', items: ['Which service orders have been open longest, and for which customers?', 'Which items come back for repair most often?'] },
  { area: 'Manufacturing', items: ['Which work orders are behind schedule this week?', 'Which components are short for the orders due next month?'] },
  { area: 'Finance', items: ['Which customers have invoices more than 60 days overdue?', 'How do this month’s sales compare with the same month last year, by product group?'] },
];

const siteUrl = new URL(url('/'), Astro.site).href;

const emailSubject = 'Could we try an AI assistant on our ERP data?';

const summary = `Hi,

I came across mcp-server-db2i, an open-source, self-hosted tool that lets AI assistants such as Claude answer questions about our ERP data in plain language. It only reads data, and people sign in with their own credentials.

I'd like to use it for the one-off questions we don't have reports for. Could you take a look and let me know if we could try it?

The main points for you:

What is enforced
- Read-only: a read-only database connection and a SQL validator that only accepts queries.
- A query timeout cancels long-running statements on the IBM i.
- A library allowlist limits which schemas can be queried or browsed. Sensitive columns can be masked.
- Every tool call is written to an audit log (JSON lines, SQL hashed by default).

Who sees what
- With the HTTP transport, people sign in with their own IBM i user profile (OAuth 2.1), so object authority and exit programs apply as usual. With stdio, the server uses the credentials in its configuration.
- Query results go to the AI assistant the user connects, so it should be one the company has approved.

How it connects
- Self-hosted: the server runs on a machine you choose, such as a workstation, an on-premises server or a container in the cloud.
- In the cloud, it needs a private network connection to the IBM i, such as a VPN. The IBM i ports should not be open to the internet.
- To use it from claude.ai, the server must be reachable over HTTPS. Access can be limited to Anthropic's IP range.
- The ODBC driver (default) and JT400 use the IBM i host servers. Nothing is installed on the IBM i. The ODBC driver needs unixODBC and the IBM i Access ODBC Driver on the server machine.
- Mapepire over SSH works when only port 22 is open. It copies its server JAR to the user's home directory on first use.

Overview: ${siteUrl}
Quickstart: ${links.quickstart}
Security: ${links.security}

It is free and MIT licensed, so there is nothing to buy.

Thanks!`;
---
<Base
  title="For business teams"
  description="Ask new questions of the ERP data on your IBM i in plain language, next to the screens, reports, Excel workbooks and BI tools you already use."
  starterQuestions={[
    'What does my IT team need to set up?',
    'How is our data kept read-only and private?',
    'Can everyone sign in with their own user?',
  ]}
>

  <section class="hero">
    <div class="container">
      <p class="label">For business teams</p>
      <h1>For the questions that don't have a report yet.</h1>
      <p class="lead">
        If your ERP runs on IBM i (many still call it AS/400), years of orders, customers, items and invoices are already there.
        Your team can ask about them in plain language, in an AI assistant such as Claude, and get the answer
        from live data. Your reports and tools stay as they are.
      </p>
      <div class="btn-row">
        <a class="btn btn--primary" href={links.docs} data-ask-docs="business-hero">Ask the docs</a>
        <a class="link-rule" href="#send-to-it">Send this to your IT team</a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="sec-head">
        <p class="label">Today</p>
        <div>
          <h2>Next to what you already have.</h2>
          <p class="lead">Companies with an ERP like yours usually get their answers in one of three ways. Each one handles the questions it was built for.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="cols3">
        <div><p class="label">01</p><h3>ERP screens and reports</h3><p class="muted">The system your team works in every day, with the reports that were built for it.</p></div>
        <div><p class="label">02</p><h3>Excel linked to the ERP</h3><p class="muted">Workbooks that pull data straight from the ERP, so the numbers land in a sheet your team can work with.</p></div>
        <div><p class="label">03</p><h3>A data warehouse or BI tool</h3><p class="muted">ERP data copied into a warehouse or data lake, with dashboards and reports on top.</p></div>
      </div>
      <p class="bridge">
        Then there is the one-off question, such as why a large customer ordered less this month.
        This is for those. It reads the ERP's own database directly. It doesn't connect to your reports,
        spreadsheets or warehouse, and it doesn't replace them. If your reports use their own
        definitions, such as what counts as an open order, your IT team can give the assistant the same ones.
      </p>
      <p class="erp-note">
        Works with any business data in Db2 for i: ERPs such as JD Edwards, Infor LX, XA and System21, SAP on IBM i
        and Iptor DC1, and also banking, insurance, warehouse and in-house systems. Not affiliated with any of these
        vendors; product names are trademarks of their owners.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section section--tight">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="sec-head">
        <p class="label">Examples</p>
        <div>
          <h2>The kind of questions people ask.</h2>
          <p class="lead">Your team types a question. The assistant finds where the data is, looks it up, and answers with the numbers.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="groups">
        {questions.map((q) => (
          <div class="group">
            <p class="label">{q.area}</p>
            <ul class="rows">{q.items.map((item) => <li>“{item}”</li>)}</ul>
          </div>
        ))}
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section section--tight">
    <div class="container two">
      <div>
        <p class="label">Definitions</p>
        <h2 class="h2s">Your definitions, not the assistant's guesses.</h2>
        <p>
          Every company has its own meaning of “open order”, “active customer” or “on time”. Your IT team can write
          those definitions down once: what a status code means, which data belongs together, and how to answer common
          questions such as “late purchase orders”.
        </p>
        <p>
          The assistant then uses your definitions instead of guessing. The logic that already lives in your reports and
          Excel queries is a good place to start.
        </p>
        <a class="link-rule" href={links.customTools}>How business definitions work <span aria-hidden="true">→</span></a>
      </div>
      <div>
        <p class="label">Safeguards</p>
        <h2 class="h2s">What it can and can't do with your data.</h2>
        <ul class="rows ticks">
          <li><strong>It only reads.</strong> It can look data up but never change it, and every request is checked before it runs. If a question takes too long, it is stopped automatically so it doesn't keep your system busy.</li>
          <li><strong>Everyone signs in as themselves.</strong> People sign in with their own user on the system, often the same user name and password as for the ERP. The system decides what each person can read.</li>
          <li><strong>You choose what is visible.</strong> Your IT team decides which parts of the ERP data it can see, and can hide sensitive fields such as personal details.</li>
          <li><strong>Everything is logged.</strong> Every request is recorded, so your IT team can see what was asked.</li>
          <li><strong>No copy of your database.</strong> Nothing is copied into a new database. The answer to each question goes to the AI assistant you use, so use one your company has approved.</li>
        </ul>
        <a class="link-rule" href={links.security}>Security details <span aria-hidden="true">→</span></a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section section--tight">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="sec-head">
        <p class="label">Good to know</p>
        <div><h2>Before you start.</h2></div>
      </div>
      <div class="cols3">
        <div><p class="label">Setup</p><h3>Someone sets it up once</h3><p class="muted">Your IT team sets it up. After that, people only add a link in their assistant and sign in.</p></div>
        <div><p class="label">Scope</p><h3>It is not your financial reporting</h3><p class="muted">Official figures still come from the reports your finance team relies on. This is for exploring and for the questions in between.</p></div>
        <div><p class="label">Quality</p><h3>Better notes, better answers</h3><p class="muted">The assistant is only as good as what it knows about your data. A few definitions make a big difference.</p></div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section section--tight" id="send-to-it">
    <div class="container two next">
      <div>
        <p class="label">Next step</p>
        <h2 class="h2s">Share it with your IT team.</h2>
        <p>
          Ask the documentation assistant anything about setup, security or what it can do. Or send the email on the
          right to your IT team. It is free and open source, with nothing to buy.
        </p>
        <div class="btn-row">
          <a class="btn btn--primary" href={links.docs} data-ask-docs="business-next">Ask the docs</a>
          <a class="link-rule" href={url('/blog')}>Read the blog</a>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="panel share">
        <div class="share-head">
          <span class="label">Email to your IT team</span>
          <button type="button" class="copy" data-copy-summary>Copy email</button>
        </div>
        <p class="subject"><span class="label">Subject</span> {emailSubject}</p>
        <pre id="summary">{summary}</pre>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</Base>

<script>
  const button = document.querySelector<HTMLButtonElement>('[data-copy-summary]');
  button?.addEventListener('click', async () => {
    const text = document.getElementById('summary')?.textContent ?? '';
    try {
      await navigator.clipboard.writeText(text);
      button.textContent = 'Copied';
    } catch {
      button.textContent = 'Select and copy';
    }
    setTimeout(() => (button.textContent = 'Copy email'), 1600);
  });
</script>

<style>
  .hero { padding-block: 112px 72px; }
  .hero h1 { margin: 18px 0 28px; max-width: 16ch; }
  .hero .lead { margin-bottom: 36px; }
  .cols3 { display: grid; border-top: 1px solid var(--line-strong); }
  @media (min-width: 900px) { .cols3 { grid-template-columns: repeat(3, 1fr); } }
  .cols3 > div { display: grid; gap: 10px; align-content: start; padding: 22px 0 28px; }
  .cols3 > div + div { border-top: 1px solid var(--line); }
  @media (min-width: 900px) {
    .cols3 > div { padding-right: 28px; }
    .cols3 > div + div { border-top: 0; border-left: 1px solid var(--line); padding-left: 28px; }
  }
  .cols3 h3 { margin: 0; font-size: 1.4rem; font-weight: 400; letter-spacing: -0.02em; }
  .cols3 p.muted { margin: 0; }
  .bridge { max-width: 52ch; margin-top: var(--space-7); font-size: 1.3rem; line-height: 1.45; letter-spacing: -0.01em; }
  .erp-note { max-width: 70ch; margin: var(--space-5) 0 0; font-size: 0.9rem; color: var(--muted); }
  .groups { border-top: 1px solid var(--line-strong); }
  .group { display: grid; gap: 8px 64px; padding-block: 20px 24px; border-bottom: 1px solid var(--line); }
  @media (min-width: 1000px) { .group { grid-template-columns: 200px minmax(0, 1fr); } }
  .group .rows li { padding: 8px 0; font-size: 1.1rem; }
  .group .rows li:first-child { padding-top: 0; }
  .two { display: grid; grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr); gap: var(--space-7); border-top: 1px solid var(--line-strong); padding-top: var(--space-6); }
  @media (min-width: 900px) { .two { grid-template-columns: repeat(2, minmax(0, 1fr)); gap: 64px; } }
  .two > div > .label { margin-bottom: var(--space-4); }
  .h2s { font-size: clamp(1.8rem, 3.4vw, 2.6rem); max-width: 18ch; margin-bottom: var(--space-5); }
  .two p { max-width: 58ch; }
  .ticks { margin: 0 0 var(--space-5); }
  .ticks li { padding: 12px 0 12px 22px; position: relative; }
  .ticks li::before { content: ''; position: absolute; left: 0; top: 20px; width: 7px; height: 7px; background: var(--accent); }
  .share { align-self: start; }
  .share-head { display: flex; justify-content: space-between; align-items: center; margin-bottom: var(--space-3); }
  .copy { font: 500 11px/1 var(--font-mono); letter-spacing: 0.08em; text-transform: uppercase; background: var(--fg); color: var(--bg); border: 0; border-radius: var(--radius-btn); padding: 8px 12px; cursor: pointer; }
  .copy:hover { background: var(--accent); color: var(--on-accent); }
  .subject { margin: 0 0 var(--space-3); padding-bottom: var(--space-3); border-bottom: 1px solid var(--line); font-size: 0.9rem; }
  .subject .label { margin-right: 8px; }
  .share pre { margin: 0; padding: 0; border: 0; background: none; white-space: pre-wrap; overflow-wrap: anywhere; font-size: 0.8rem; }
</style>
